    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d308">

                <pb facs="0536" n="404" sameAs="frus:frus1945Berlinv01/pg404"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Department of the Army Files: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 308<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Representative on the Allied Control
                        Commission for Bulgaria
                    </hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Crane</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Commanding
                        General, United States Army Forces, Mediterranean Theater of Operations</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >McNarney</hi>)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d308-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Sofia</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-07-10">10 July [19]45</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    CG
                    MTOUSA
                    ACC Hungary and ACC
                    Rumania US Delegations from ACC
                    Bulgaria US. Msg
                    Nr 1906.</p>
                <p>More than two months have passed since the end of hostilities with Germany.
                    According to the terms of the Armistice with Bulgaria,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d308-f2"/> there should have been
                    certain changes in the operation of the ACC. No
                    changes have taken place.</p>
                <p>I am unable to find out what is being done in the name of the ACC.</p>
                <p>Complete control is still being exercised by the Russians over our plane and
                    personnel entry into Bulgaria for no reason
                    whatever. This includes officials and employees of our Government. We are still
                    confined to Sofia unless under escort of a Russian officer.</p>
                <p>Our position here is most embarrassing. People, Bulgarians and <pb facs="0537"
                        n="405" xml:id="pg405"/> representatives of other governments, openly
                    comment on our apparent helplessness before the power of Russia.</p>
                <p>In view of our treatment it is difficult to explain the fact that we are still
                    furnishing them with lend-lease supplies, and are apparently preparing to lend
                    them $6,000,000,000.</p>
                <p>The failure to reach any decision in the Dimitroff case is greatly strengthening the hands of the local
                    Communists.</p>
                <p>Incidentally Barnes is becoming much
                    harassed by the long visit, over six weeks now.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d308-f3"/>
                </p>
                <p>Can’t we talk a little tough to the Russians in Moscow and avoid being kicked in
                    the face every day?</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d308-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d308-f1-content">
                <p>Received by the United States Delegation to the Berlin Conference on July 13 as a
                    retransmission from the War Department.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d308-f2" n="2" xml:id="d308-f2-content">
                <p>Signed at Moscow, October 28, 1944 (Executive Agreement Series No. 437; 58 Stat. (2) 1498).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d308-f3" n="3" xml:id="d308-f3-content">
                <p>
                    Georgy M. Dimitrov, leader of the
                    Bulgarian National Agrarian Union, had sought refuge in Barnes’ home at Sofia.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
